WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.009 --> 00:00:09.070
This is me driving in what I think is one
of the most bizarre places in the world.

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:11.879
I just crossed over from Israel into the West
Bank.

00:00:11.879 --> 00:00:17.060
If you look at a map of where I am right now,
you will see a jumbled mess of of Palestinian

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:21.680
towns shown here in green, and Israeli
settlements, which are blue.

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:23.750
Many people think of this territory as Palestine.

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:29.600
But of the 3 million people living out here,
almost twenty percent of them are Jewish Israeli citizens.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:32.220
The Israelis living out here are called settlers.

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:35.300
They live in the West Bank but are citizens
of Israel.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:40.260
As I drive I’m looking at effectively two
different nations, woven into each other through

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:44.340
decades of conflict,
I visited 15 settlements all over the West

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:49.140
Bank, talking to the people who have decided
to pack up and move to middle of this disputed land.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:51.720
We’ll meet them in coming videos.

00:00:51.729 --> 00:01:07.299
But first let’s look at the maps that explain
how the West Bank got to looking like this.

00:01:07.299 --> 00:01:11.080
So let’s first go back to 1948, when the
map looks a lot different.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:14.110
Back then, all this land was controlled by
Great Britain.

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:18.640
Due to growing tension between Jews and Arabs,
the UN worked with the Britain to split the

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:24.610
land into two states, one for Jews, Israel,
and one for Arabs, Palestine.

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:29.460
The Jews accepted the plan and declared independence.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:33.660
But the Arab states in the region saw this
plan as just more European colonialism.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:37.880
They didn’t accept the plan and declared
war with Israel.

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:41.740
Israel won the war, pushing well past the
borders of the UN plan.

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:45.800
During the peace negotiations, a ceasefire
line was drawn in green ink.

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:47.780
It became known as the green line.

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:53.641
It wasn’t a border, just a ceasefire line
with this being the state of Israel, and this

00:01:53.641 --> 00:01:57.630
section being controlled by Jordan, who had
taken control of it during the war they just fought.

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:03.060
The Jordanians named this newly-seized land
the West Bank because it was West of the Jordan River.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:09.060
The fragile ceasefire remained until 1967
when Israel fought another war with its Arab neighbors.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:14.420
Israel wasn’t looking to take over land
in the war, but In just six days of fighting,

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:20.530
it blew past the Green Line and seized a whole
swath of land, including the entire West Bank.

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:24.920
Suddenly Israel had a decision to make: do
they make the West Bank a part of Israel and

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:29.680
give the 1.1 million Arabs living there Israeli
citizenship and voting rights?

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:34.430
Do they give back the land to their enemy
Jordan or else let the people create their

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:36.170
own Palestinian state?

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:38.969
This became a major debate in Israeli politics.

00:02:38.969 --> 00:02:44.249
Many Israelis saw this war they just won not
only as a military victory but a religious

00:02:44.249 --> 00:02:49.489
sign that the Jews were meant to return to
the the place where a huge amount of Jewish

00:02:49.489 --> 00:02:54.209
ancient history happened, the hills of ancient
Judea and Samaria, which was basically the

00:02:54.209 --> 00:02:55.279
entire West Bank.

00:02:55.279 --> 00:03:00.360
So while the government was debating what
to do, Israeli civilians began moving into

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:03.329
the West Bank without any permission from
the government.

00:03:03.329 --> 00:03:07.689
They just starting setting up homes, establishing
a Jewish presence in this region.

00:03:07.689 --> 00:03:12.480
Suddenly, any debate about what to do with
the West Bank had to take this growing number

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:14.180
of Israeli civilians into consideration.

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:15.180
This is how the the settler movement was born.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.439
The rest of the world did not approve of this.

00:03:17.439 --> 00:03:21.599
As the settler presence grew, the UN issued
a resolution saying that the settlements had

00:03:21.599 --> 00:03:27.099
“...no legal validity and constitute a serious
obstruction to achieving a comprehensive,

00:03:27.099 --> 00:03:29.159
just and lasting peace in the Middle East.”

00:03:29.159 --> 00:03:37.690
Two different narratives emerged here: One
said Jewish civilians were moving onto mostly

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:42.560
empty plots of land that they had captured
in a war and that had deep historical and

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:44.560
spiritual significance to them.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:48.780
The other side, which is the side most of
the world took, said that these settlers were

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:50.939
colonizing land to expand their nation.

00:03:50.939 --> 00:03:51.939
The settler project was widely seen as apart
of an illegal occupation of the West Bank.

00:03:51.939 --> 00:03:57.569
In spite of international condemnation, the
number of settlers grew quickly.

00:03:57.569 --> 00:04:01.730
Over the next few decades, more and more factions
of the Israeli government began to support

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:06.540
the settler movement, allocating public resources
and granting permits for building.

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:10.439
The Israeli housing ministry and military
began developing plans for how to build up

00:04:10.439 --> 00:04:11.620
the West Bank.

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:16.060
They built roads throughout the entire region,
allowing for easy access between settlements

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:17.470
and mainland Israel.

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:21.070
More and more building permits were given
out and new planned communities began popping

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:22.760
up all over the West Bank.

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:28.220
The settlements slowly shifted from a fringe
group of motivated civilians to an institutionalized

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:33.170
part of Israeli society, totally supported
by the state.

00:04:33.170 --> 00:04:35.140
Here are the Palestinian towns in the West
Bank.

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:37.670
As settler activity surged in the 80s and
90s, watch how the settlements weave around

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:40.840
these towns.

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:42.180
Palestinians didn’t like this encroachment.

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:46.230
They began protesting, sometimes with extreme
violence.

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:50.160
Between the violence and the condemnation
from the international community of the settlements,

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:52.440
the situation became unsustainable.

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:58.380
So in the mid 90s American president Bill
Clinton, Israeli Prime Minister Yitzhak Rabin,

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:03.180
and Palestinian leader Yasser Arafat signed
the Oslo Accords, agreements that established

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:07.370
Palestinian government and split the West
Bank into 3 sections.

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:12.620
Area A gave Palestinians total control over
security and government.

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:17.860
This makes up about 18% of the West Bank but
most of the palestinian population centers.

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:23.060
This gave the Palestinian government self
rule for the first time.

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:29.200
Area B was designated for Palestinian government
control while retaining Israeli security control,

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:32.440
meaning the Israeli military remains very
present.

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:35.220
Area B is about 22% of the West Bank.

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:39.840
Area C Remained completely under Israeli military
and government control.

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:41.340
This is where all the settlements are.

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:45.190
It is about 60% of the West Bank.

00:05:45.190 --> 00:05:48.590
So this is how we ended up with this mess
of a map.

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:53.370
Israelis can easily come and go to mainland
Israel through really nice roads that go straight

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:54.370
to settlements.

00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:59.270
They call these “flyovers” because they
bypass Palestinian villages and give easy

00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:04.040
access from one settlement to another, although
not every settlement has a flyover road like this.

00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:07.700
Palestinians can drive on almost all roads
in the West Bank but their movement it often

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:14.330
more difficult, having to stop at checkpoints
or have their car inspected by a soldier.

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:21.800
But perhaps the biggest difficulty faced by
palestinians is how restricted their economy

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:24.450
is due to this carved up land.

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:30.620
Area C contains the majority of the West Banks
agricultural land, as well as water and mineral resources.

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:36.120
Palestinians companies are severely restricted
in accessing these resources which takes a

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:38.220
huge hit on their economy.

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:42.910
So with these three sections agreed upon by
both sides, the settlements continued to grow

00:06:42.910 --> 00:06:47.950
within Area C. But in 2005 something happened
that would ignite even more passion for the

00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:53.840
settler movement., Israeli Prime Minister
Ariel Sharon decided to remove 8,500 settlers

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:58.490
from the Gaza strip, another disputed area.

00:06:58.490 --> 00:07:04.480
Seeing Israelis being evicted, their homes
demolished, left a huge mark on the country,

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:06.261
especially the settlers.

00:07:06.261 --> 00:07:09.300
They immediately redoubled their efforts to
settle the West Bank.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:12.340
The numbers continued to grow.

00:07:12.340 --> 00:07:16.890
Most people who think about resolution to
this conflict propose a two state solution,

00:07:16.890 --> 00:07:20.770
meaning giving the Palestinians a state somewhere
in the West Bank region.

00:07:20.770 --> 00:07:25.930
But if you look at this map you can see what
it’s getting harder and harder to do that.

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:29.050
The settlers living in Area C of the West
Bank are not living in tents or Caravans.

00:07:29.050 --> 00:07:35.820
They are living in developed communities with
schools, hospitals and even a university.

00:07:35.820 --> 00:08:03.409
In the next video I will go inside the settlements
and talk to the people living there.

